<Guide slug="what-is-a-zip-code" description="A ZIP code is the 5-digit code USPS uses to route mail. Learn its history since 1963, what each digit means, and the four types of ZIP code." faqs={faqs}>
  <h1>What is a ZIP code?</h1>
  <p class="lead" data-answer>A <strong>ZIP code</strong> is the five-digit number the United States Postal Service (USPS) uses to sort and route mail to a specific area. “ZIP” stands for <strong>Zone Improvement Plan</strong>, and the system has been in use since <strong>July 1, 1963</strong>.</p>

  <h2>A short history</h2>
  <p>Before 1963, mail was sorted by hand using city and state names, plus two-digit “postal zones” in about 125 large cities introduced in 1943. As mail volume exploded after World War II, the Post Office Department needed a code that machines — and clerks — could read at a glance. The five-digit ZIP code rolled out nationwide in 1963, promoted by a cartoon mascot called Mr. ZIP.</p>
  <p>In 1983 USPS added <a href="/guides/what-is-zip-plus-4/">ZIP+4</a>, four extra digits that identify a block face, building or group of PO boxes.</p>

  <h2>What the five digits mean</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>1st digit</strong> — a national area, from 0 in the Northeast to 9 on the West Coast.</li>
    <li><strong>2nd and 3rd digits</strong> — a sectional center facility (SCF), the processing plant that serves a region.</li>
    <li><strong>4th and 5th digits</strong> — the individual post office or delivery area.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For example, in <a href="/zip/90210/">90210</a> (Beverly Hills, CA), the <strong>9</strong> means the Pacific states and <strong>902</strong> is the SCF that serves that part of Los Angeles County. See the full breakdown in our <a href="/guides/us-zip-code-format/">ZIP code format guide</a>.</p>

  <h2>Four types of ZIP code</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Standard</strong> — ordinary street delivery to homes and businesses.</li>
    <li><strong>PO Box</strong> — used only for post office boxes at a particular post office.</li>
    <li><strong>Unique</strong> — assigned to one high-volume address, such as a government agency, university or large company.</li>
    <li><strong>Military</strong> — APO, FPO and DPO addresses for overseas service members.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>ZIP codes vs. ZIP Code Tabulation Areas</h2>
  <p>A ZIP code is really a set of delivery routes, not a shape on a map. To publish statistics by ZIP code, the US Census Bureau builds <strong>ZIP Code Tabulation Areas (ZCTAs)</strong> — area approximations of those routes. The population, income and land-area figures on this site come from ZCTAs, which is why they can differ slightly from USPS boundaries.</p>

  <h2>Fun facts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The lowest ZIP code is <strong>00501</strong>, a unique ZIP for the IRS in Holtsville, New York.</li>
    <li>The highest is <a href="/zip/99950/">99950</a>, Ketchikan, Alaska.</li>
    <li>See which ZIP codes are the <a href="/rankings/richest-zip-codes/">richest</a>, <a href="/rankings/most-populated-zip-codes/">most populated</a> and <a href="/rankings/largest-zip-codes/">largest</a> in the country.</li>
  </ul>
